      `the seller is at fault` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the buyer is at fault` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 9(2) the action for damages against the party at fault applies` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the third party is prevented from making the valuation` AND (`the seller is at fault` OR `the buyer is at fault`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the third party is prevented from making the valuation by the fault of the seller or buyer" -- ruled 2026-11-05 (b): a "where" condition not met is the text's no, like "if"
            JUST FALSE

§§ `Conditions and warranties (ss 10 to 15A)`

-- s 10(1).  Stipulations as to time of payment are not of the essence, unless a different intention appears.
-- src:323 | 10.—(1) Unless a different intention appears from the terms of the
-- src:325 | contract, stipulations as to time of payment are not of the essence of a
-- src:326 | contract of sale.
@ref s 10(1) (src:323-326)
GIVEN `a different intention appears from the terms of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10(1) a stipulation as to time of payment is of the essence of the contract` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `a different intention appears from the terms of the contract`
        THEN
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Unless a different intention appears from the terms of the contract" -- the subsection then says nothing, and s 10(2) leaves it to the terms.
            NOTHING
        OTHERWISE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "stipulations as to time of payment are not of the essence of a contract of sale"
            JUST FALSE

-- s 10(2).  Whether any other stipulation as to time is of the essence depends on the terms of the contract.
-- src:328 | (2) Whether any other stipulation as to time is or is not of the
-- src:329 | essence of the contract depends on the terms of the contract.
@ref s 10(2) (src:328-329)
GIVEN `the terms of the contract make the stipulation as to time of the essence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10(2) any other stipulation as to time is of the essence of the contract` IS
    CONSIDER `the terms of the contract make the stipulation as to time of the essence`
    WHEN JUST answer THEN JUST answer
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "depends on the terms of the contract", which are not among the sources; the person states what the terms do.
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 10(3).  "Month" prima facie means calendar month.  The presumption gives way if the contrary is shown.
-- src:331 | (3) In a contract of sale, “month” prima facie means calendar
-- src:332 | month.
@ref s 10(3) (src:331-332)
GIVEN `a contrary meaning of "month" is shown` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10(3) a month in a contract of sale means a calendar month` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `a contrary meaning of "month" is shown`
        THEN
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "prima facie" -- the presumption yields to a contrary meaning, and the subsection does not say what the contrary meaning is.
            NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 10(3).  A period of calendar months from a date ends on the corresponding day of the month, that many months on.
-- The Act says only "calendar month": where the target month has no corresponding day (31 January plus one
-- month) the Act does not say which day the period ends on, so the function answers NOTHING.
-- src:331 | (3) In a contract of sale, “month” prima facie means calendar
-- src:332 | month.
@ref s 10(3) the end of a period (src:331-332)
GIVEN `the date the period starts` IS A DATE
      `the number of calendar months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `s 10(3) the date on which a period of calendar months ends` IS
    BRANCH
        IF DATE_DAY (`the corresponding date` `the date the period starts` `the number of calendar months`) EQUALS DATE_DAY `the date the period starts`
        THEN JUST (`the corresponding date` `the date the period starts` `the number of calendar months`)
        OTHERWISE
            -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "In a contract of sale, “month” prima facie means calendar month" does not say where a period ends when the later month has no corresponding day.
            NOTHING
    WHERE
        GIVEN start IS A DATE
              months IS A NUMBER
        GIVETH A DATE
        `the corresponding date` MEANS Date (DATE_DAY start) (DATE_MONTH start PLUS months) (DATE_YEAR start)

-- s 10(3).  Whether a date falls within the period of calendar months, the last day of the period included.
-- src:331 | (3) In a contract of sale, “month” prima facie means calendar
-- src:332 | month.
@ref s 10(3) within the period (src:331-332)
GIVEN `the date the period starts` IS A DATE
      `the number of calendar months` IS A NUMBER
      `the date asked about` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10(3) the date is within the period of calendar months` IS
    CONSIDER `s 10(3) the date on which a period of calendar months ends` `the date the period starts` `the number of calendar months`
    WHEN JUST endDate THEN JUST (`the date asked about` AT MOST endDate)
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the end of the period is not given by the Act, so no date can be placed inside or outside it.
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 11(1), first limb.  The buyer may waive a condition to be fulfilled by the seller.
-- src:335 | 11.—(1) Where a contract of sale is subject to a condition to be
-- src:337 | fulfilled by the seller, the buyer may waive the condition, or may elect
@ref s 11(1) waiver (src:335-337)
GIVEN `the contract of sale is subject to a condition to be fulfilled by the seller` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 11(1) the buyer may waive the condition` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the contract of sale is subject to a condition to be fulfilled by the seller`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the power is given "Where a contract of sale is subject to a condition to be fulfilled by the seller". -- ruled 2026-11-05 (b): a "where" condition not met is the text's no, like "if"
            JUST FALSE

-- s 11(1), second limb.  The buyer may elect to treat the breach as a breach of warranty.
-- src:335 | 11.—(1) Where a contract of sale is subject to a condition to be
-- src:337 | fulfilled by the seller, the buyer may waive the condition, or may elect
-- src:342 | to treat the breach of the condition as a breach of warranty and not as a
-- src:343 | ground for treating the contract as repudiated.
@ref s 11(1) election (src:335-343)
GIVEN `the contract of sale is subject to a condition to be fulfilled by the seller` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the seller has broken that condition` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 11(1) the buyer may elect to treat the breach of the condition as a breach of warranty` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the contract of sale is subject to a condition to be fulfilled by the seller` AND `the seller has broken that condition`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the election is given for "the breach of the condition" where the contract is "subject to a condition to be fulfilled by the seller". -- ruled 2026-11-05 (b): a "where" condition not met is the text's no, like "if"
            JUST FALSE

-- s 11(2).  Condition or warranty depends on the construction of the contract; the label does not decide it.
-- src:345 | (2) Whether a stipulation in a contract of sale is a condition, the
-- src:346 | breach of which may give rise to a right to treat the contract as
-- src:347 | repudiated, or a warranty, the breach of which may give rise to a claim
-- src:348 | for damages but not to a right to reject the goods and treat the contract
-- src:349 | as repudiated, depends in each case on the construction of the
-- src:350 | contract; and a stipulation may be a condition, though called a
-- src:351 | warranty in the contract.
@ref s 11(2) (src:345-351)
GIVEN `the label the contract gives the stipulation` IS A `Label the contract gives a stipulation`
      `how the contract is construed` IS A `Construction of a stipulation`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of stipulation`
DECIDE `s 11(2) the stipulation is a condition or a warranty` IS
    -- The label is read and deliberately not used: "a stipulation may be a condition, though called a warranty in the contract".
    CONSIDER `how the contract is construed`
    WHEN `construed as a condition` THEN JUST `a condition`
    WHEN `construed as a warranty` THEN JUST `a warranty`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "depends in each case on the construction of the contract", which is not among the sources.
    WHEN `not yet construed` THEN NOTHING

-- s 11(3).  After acceptance, in a contract that is not severable, a breach of condition by the seller can
-- only be treated as a breach of warranty, unless the contract says otherwise; subject to s 35A.
-- src:353 | (3) Subject to section 35A, where a contract of sale is not severable
-- src:354 | and the buyer has accepted the goods or part of them, the breach of a
-- src:355 | condition to be fulfilled by the seller can only be treated as a breach of
-- src:356 | warranty, and not as a ground for rejecting the goods and treating the
-- src:357 | contract as repudiated, unless there is an express or implied term of
-- src:358 | the contract to that effect.
@ref s 11(3) (src:353-358)
GIVEN `the contract of sale is severable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the buyer has accepted the goods or part of them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an express or implied term of the contract allows rejection` IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 35A preserves the buyer's right to reject` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 11(3) the breach of a condition can only be treated as a breach of warranty` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the contract of sale is severable`) AND `the buyer has accepted the goods or part of them`
        THEN
            BRANCH
                IF `section 35A preserves the buyer's right to reject`
                THEN
                    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subject to section 35A" -- s 35A leaves the buyer's right to reject the rest.
                    -- MARGINAL (a): "Subject to" is read like "unless": when s 35A applies the "can only be treated as a breach of warranty" rule does not hold.
                    JUST FALSE
                IF `an express or implied term of the contract allows rejection`
                THEN
                    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless there is an express or implied term of the contract to that effect"
                    -- MARGINAL (a): "unless" is read as the text's no when the express or implied term exists.
                    JUST FALSE
                OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the subsection applies "where a contract of sale is not severable and the buyer has accepted the goods or part of them". -- ruled 2026-11-05 (b): a "where" condition not met is the text's no, like "if"
            JUST FALSE

-- s 11(4).
-- src:360 | (4) Nothing in this section affects a condition or warranty whose
-- src:361 | fulfilment is excused by law by reason of impossibility or otherwise.
@ref s 11(4) (src:360-361)
GIVEN `the fulfilment of the condition or warranty is excused by law by reason of impossibility or otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 11(4) section 11 affects the condition or warranty` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the fulfilment of the condition or warranty is excused by law by reason of impossibility or otherwise`
        THEN
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this section affects a condition or warranty whose fulfilment is excused by law by reason of impossibility or otherwise."
            JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE
            -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of a condition or warranty whose fulfilment is excused by law.
            NOTHING

-- s 12(3).  The contract in which the seller means to transfer only such title as he or a third person may have.
